  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-108501",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x>0,$ we have $y>0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14231262/assets/8b2d1487d180db1df306cd8c83088ef0334d29769bd7ef94eed7f75d4e6cedf7.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y>0,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4{e}^{-4\\cdot y/2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2y}, & \\,y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
